 86	The called three-argument OpenZeppelin routine is not merely suggestively named: its implementation specifies floor division, forms the full 512-bit product, and in the non-overflow branch returns integer division directly. In the 512-bit branch it subtracts `mulmod`'s remainder before completing the exact division (`Math.sol:L227-L275`).
 87	
 88	```solidity
 89	// target/packages/contracts/node_modules/@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/math/Math.sol:L199-L216
 90	/**
 91	 * @dev Calculates floor(x * y / denominator) with full precision. Throws if result overflows a uint256 or
 92	 * denominator == 0.
 93	 */
 94	function mulDiv(uint256 x, uint256 y, uint256 denominator) internal pure returns (uint256 result) {
 95	    unchecked {
 96	        (uint256 high, uint256 low) = mul512(x, y);
 97	
 98	        // Handle non-overflow cases, 256 by 256 division.
 99	        if (high == 0) {
100	            // Solidity will revert if denominator == 0, unlike the div opcode on its own.
101	            // The surrounding unchecked block does not change this fact.
102	            // See https://docs.soliditylang.org/en/latest/control-structures.html#checked-or-unchecked-arithmetic.
103	            return low / denominator;
104	        }
105	```
